{"t":1793947372,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"cf335b07-19a2-4b88-8657-c842c00c23ae","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-06T02:33:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 060233\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n933 PM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nChances for thunderstorms along the I-95 corridor late tonight\nlook to be trending upward, with the greatest chance between\nroughly 2 and 7 AM.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Unseasonable warmth and rain chances through Friday. \n\n- 2) Seasonably cool weather returns this weekend.\n\n- 3) Dry conditions early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unseasonable warmth and rain chances through\nFriday.\n\nAs of 9 PM, a band of showers was ongoing along the I-95\ncorridor from Fairfax County northeastward into Northeast\nMaryland. These showers will continue to lift northeastward over\nthe next few hours. There may be a bit of a lull following that\nactivity, but shower/thunderstorm activity is expected to\nincrease in coverage again from Central Virginia northeastward\nthrough the I-95 corridor later tonight as a wave of low \npressure develops along a stalled out frontal boundary. Lift \nwill increase through the night via continued warm/moist low- \nlevel advection and convergence, as well as forcing for ascent \nahead of the mid/upper trough and jet streak. This will likely \nresult in a broken band of showers and a few thunderstorms near \nand east of I-95, especially later tonight. PWATs will remain in\nthe 1.4-1.7\" range near and east of I-95 with heavy downpours \npossible. The 00z IAD sounding had 1552 J/kg of MUCAPE, and \naround 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE will persist through the night with \ncooling aloft occurring atop the unseasonably warm and moist \nairmass in place at the surface. While some lightning- producing\nupdrafts of moderate intensity are plausible, the risk for \nsevere weather (tornadoes, hail, or damaging winds) is very low \ndue to a small stable layer near the surface. The risk for \nflooding is also low due to recent dry conditions. Some CAM \nruns show isolated totals of 1-3\" along and southeast of I-95. \nThat likely wouldn't cause any issues, unless the absolute \nhighest values fall directly over the urban areas. As of now, \nthe most likely location for the highest totals looks to be \nacross Southern Maryland.\n\nThe front will slowly begin to shift southeastward and out of\nthe area on Friday as a wave of low pressure develops and lifts\nnorth-northeast away from the region. Rain chances linger over\nsouthern Maryland into early Friday evening, with a few\nsprinkles or showers possible further west (especially along and\nwest of the Allegheny Front in upslope) as the upper trough\nswings through. Temperatures may cool enough to result in a mix\nor change to spotty light snow late Friday night into Saturday\nmorning for elevations above 3500 feet in the Appalachians as\nconditions dry and cool elsewhere on northwest breezes. These\nbreezes will usher in seasonably cooler air for the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably cool weather returns this weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will build near and south of the region this\nweekend. Northwest breezes are likely to stay elevated for many\nlocations (higher elevations, northern and eastern piedmont, the\nmetros, and over/near larger waterways like the Chesapeake Bay\nand tidal Potomac River). Closer to the high in the central\nShenandoah Valley into the central Virginia piedmont, winds may\ndecouple Saturday night and especially Sunday night resulting in\nthe potential for near freezing temperatures. The growing season\nremains active for all areas east of the Blue Ridge/Catoctin\nMountains except for western Loudoun County in Virginia, and \nFrederick County in Maryland. It is possible that patchy frost\nmay develop late Saturday night into early Sunday morning across\nareas the decouple in central Virginia, but frost/near freezing\ntemperatures are more likely Sunday night into Monday morning.\nStill, with the center of the high pressure to the south, there\nmay be enough of a light wind to keep the I-95 corridor and\nnorthern-to-central Maryland to prevent frost/freeze in these \nlocations. Otherwise, outside of some lingering light upslope\nalong and west of the Allegheny Front Saturday, the weekend\nlooks to turn out dry.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry conditions early next week.\n\nAfter a brief wet and humid period late this week, conditions \nstart to dry out again this weekend in the wake of a cold \nfront. Upper-level ridging settles in overhead by Monday with \ngenerally clear conditions anticipated, although winds could \nremain breezy as the front transitions offshore. As upper-level\nridging transitions to zonal flow in the following couple of \ndays, conditions should continue to remain benign, with \ndiminishing winds by mid week.\n\nAfternoon highs drop back into the lower 60s in the immediate \naftermath of the front, but gradually increase again through \nWednesday as high pressure lingers. Expect somewhat above \nseasonable temperatures to persist as this dry pattern\ncontinues, though there are hints of a pattern shift toward the\nend of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /02Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nCeilings are forecast to drop to IFR later tonight, while\ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms simultaneously increases.\nAs of now, the highest chance for thunderstorms looks to be\nbetween 4 AM and 7 AM, although a storm can't be ruled out a bit\nearlier or later. That potential has been highlighted at DCA,\nBWI, and MTN with PROB30 groups. Chances for storms decrease the\nfurther west one goes.\n\nWinds will shift to the west, northwest, and north rather\ngradually as the front slowly crosses tonight into Friday. The\nfront may even briefly stall near the I-95 corridor which could\nlead to a period of erratic wind direction (albeit at light\nspeeds) early Friday morning. As the front inches southeastward\nFriday, conditions will slowly lift to VFR. Winds likely \nincrease out of the northwest after 03Z/10PM EST Saturday as the\nfront makes a more pronounced push offshore and a wave low \npressure moves away up the coast.\n\nVFR conditions are anticipated Saturday through early next week\nas a cold front transitions offshore. Winds flow generally \nwesterly, potentially gusting up to 25 knots at times through \nMonday before diminishing later in the week.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisories remain in effect for a few more hours\nacross the widest waters of the Middle Bay. Showers and a few \nthunderstorms are expected to develop later tonight, then \npersist in waves through at least midday Friday before exiting. \nStorms should be largely elevated, though some sporadic gusty \nwinds can't completely be ruled out. Dry conditions and gusty \nnorthwest winds are then expected to develop Friday night and \npersist into the weekend.\n\nSmall Craft Advisories are likely to be needed much of the time\nin west to northwest flow late Friday night through Monday in \nthe wake of the cold front. Additional SCAs may be needed \nTuesday in the northern portions of the Chesapeake Bay. Peak\ngusts around 30 knots are possible at times.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies are running around 1 to 1.5 feet. Expect these \nto fall after a cold front crosses Friday. Until then, this \nwill bring a few locations into Action stage through tonight.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 3 AM EST Friday for ANZ534-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF/KJP/SRT\nAVIATION...DHOF/SRT\nMARINE...DHOF/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793947324,"days":{"2026-11-06":{"hi":70,"lo":47,"pop":77,"short":"Patchy Fog"},"2026-11-07":{"hi":60,"lo":45,"pop":0,"short":"Su